                <pb facs="0211" n="199" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg199"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.Consultation(2)/53: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-23T16:00:00-03:00">June 23, 1940—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:11 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>302. My telegram No. 301, June 23, 11 a.m. For the Under Secretary. Aranha now
                    says he has received a number of telegrams from his missions in other countries
                    reporting that those governments declared that they would have been glad to hold
                    conference in Rio de Janeiro but that the American representatives had “already
                    proposed Habana”.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
